---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";

const title = "What to Buy Before Your Rescue Dog Arrives | My Paws Bosnia";
const description = "A simple checklist of everything you need before bringing your rescue dog home. Essentials, food, comfort items, safety gear, and grooming basics.";

const articleSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Article",
  headline: "What to Buy Before Your Rescue Dog Arrives",
  description,
  author: { "@type": "Organization", name: "My Paws Bosnia" },
  publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "My Paws Bosnia" },
  mainEntityOfPage: "https://mypawsbosnia.org/blog/what-to-buy-before-your-rescue-dog-arrives",
};
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} lang="en" jsonLd={[articleSchema]}>
  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="bg-light-bg py-12 md:py-16">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-teal uppercase tracking-wide mb-3">Preparing for Your Dog</p>
      <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-slate-dark leading-tight">What to Buy Before Your Rescue Dog Arrives</h1>
      <p class="text-lg text-body mt-4 leading-relaxed">
        Your dog has been approved and transport is booked. Now it is time to get your home ready. You do not need to buy everything at once, but having the basics sorted before arrival day will make the transition much easier for both of you.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-12">

    <!-- Essentials -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-dark mb-4">The essentials</h2>
      <p class="text-body leading-relaxed mb-4">
        These are the things your dog will need from the very first day.
      </p>
      <ul class="space-y-2">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
          <span class="text-teal mt-0.5">&#10003;</span>
          <span class="text-body"><strong>Dog bed.</strong> Something soft and washable. Rescue dogs may not have had their own bed before, so keep it simple.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
          <span class="text-teal mt-0.5">&#10003;</span>
          <span class="text-body"><strong>Crate.</strong> A crate gives your dog a safe, quiet space to decompress. Size it so they can stand, turn around, and lie flat comfortably.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
          <span class="text-teal mt-0.5">&#10003;</span>
          <span class="text-body"><strong>Food and water bowls.</strong> Stainless steel or ceramic. Avoid plastic, which can harbour bacteria.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
          <span class="text-teal mt-0.5">&#10003;</span>
          <span class="text-body"><strong>Lead.</strong> A standard 1.5 to 2 metre lead. Avoid retractable leads for the first few weeks as they give you less control.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
          <span class="text-teal mt-0.5">&#10003;</span>
          <span class="text-body"><strong>Collar with ID tag.</strong> Your contact details and your dog's name. This is a legal requirement in most countries.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
          <span class="text-teal mt-0.5">&#10003;</span>
          <span class="text-body"><strong>Harness.</strong> Many rescue dogs have never walked on a lead. A harness is more secure and more comfortable than a collar alone, especially while your dog is still learning.</span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- Food -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-dark mb-4">Food</h2>
      <div class="bg-light-bg rounded-xl p-5 mb-4">
        <p class="text-body leading-relaxed">
          <strong>Ask your rescue first.</strong> We will tell you exactly what food your dog has been eating at the shelter so you can buy it in advance. Keeping them on the same food for the first couple of weeks helps avoid stomach upsets during an already stressful transition.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ul class="space-y-2">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
          <span class="text-teal mt-0.5">&#10003;</span>
          <span class="text-body">Buy enough of the dog's current food for at least two weeks</span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
          <span class="text-teal mt-0.5">&#10003;</span>
          <span class="text-body">If you want to switch brands later, do it gradually over 7 to 10 days by mixing the old and new food together</span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
          <span class="text-teal mt-0.5">&#10003;</span>
          <span class="text-body">Pick up some small, soft training treats for rewarding good behaviour in those early days</span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- Comfort -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-dark mb-4">Comfort items</h2>
      <p class="text-body leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Your dog has just travelled a long way and everything is new. A few comfort items go a long way.
      </p>
      <ul class="space-y-2">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
          <span class="text-teal mt-0.5">&#10003;</span>
          <span class="text-body"><strong>Blankets.</strong> Old towels or fleece blankets for the bed, the crate, and the car. Expect some accidents in the first week.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
          <span class="text-teal mt-0.5">&#10003;</span>
          <span class="text-body"><strong>Kong or chew toy.</strong> A stuffed Kong keeps anxious dogs busy and gives them something positive to focus on. Freeze it with peanut butter for longer lasting distraction.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
          <span class="text-teal mt-0.5">&#10003;</span>
          <span class="text-body"><strong>A couple of soft toys.</strong> Some dogs love them, some ignore them. Start with one or two and see what your dog enjoys.</span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- Safety -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-dark mb-4">Safety</h2>
      <p class="text-body leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Rescue dogs can be unpredictable in a new environment. A little preparation prevents problems.
      </p>
      <ul class="space-y-2">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
          <span class="text-teal mt-0.5">&#10003;</span>
          <span class="text-body"><strong>Baby gates.</strong> Useful for blocking off stairs or rooms while your dog settles in. They let your dog see and hear you without having full run of the house.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
          <span class="text-teal mt-0.5">&#10003;</span>
          <span class="text-body"><strong>Car harness or travel crate.</strong> You will need one for the first vet visit and for any car journeys. A secured crate in the boot or a crash-tested car harness both work well.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
          <span class="text-teal mt-0.5">&#10003;</span>
          <span class="text-body"><strong>Poo bags.</strong> Buy more than you think you need. Stress can cause digestive changes in the first few days.</span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- Grooming -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-dark mb-4">Grooming basics</h2>
      <p class="text-body leading-relaxed mb-4">
        You do not need a full grooming kit straight away. Just the basics to keep your dog comfortable.
      </p>
      <ul class="space-y-2">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
          <span class="text-teal mt-0.5">&#10003;</span>
          <span class="text-body">A simple brush or comb suited to your dog's coat type</span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
          <span class="text-teal mt-0.5">&#10003;</span>
          <span class="text-body">Dog shampoo (not human shampoo, which can irritate their skin)</span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
          <span class="text-teal mt-0.5">&#10003;</span>
          <span class="text-body">Nail clippers or a nail file. Your vet can also do this at the first visit if you are not confident.</span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- First vet visit -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-dark mb-4">First vet visit supplies</h2>
      <p class="text-body leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Book a vet appointment within the first week of your dog arriving. Bring these along.
      </p>
      <ul class="space-y-2">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
          <span class="text-teal mt-0.5">&#10003;</span>
          <span class="text-body">Your dog's pet passport and all veterinary paperwork from the rescue</span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
          <span class="text-teal mt-0.5">&#10003;</span>
          <span class="text-body">A list of any questions you have about your dog's health or behaviour</span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
          <span class="text-teal mt-0.5">&#10003;</span>
          <span class="text-body">Treats to reward your dog for staying calm at the vet</span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-body leading-relaxed mt-4">
        Every dog from My Paws Bosnia arrives fully vaccinated, neutered, microchipped, and with a pet passport. Your vet can register the microchip to your name and set up ongoing parasite prevention.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- CTA -->
    <section class="bg-light-bg rounded-2xl p-8 text-center">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-dark mb-3">Still looking for your dog?</h2>
      <p class="text-body mb-6">Browse our available dogs and start your application. We will help you every step of the way.</p>
      <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
        <a href="/adopt" class="inline-block px-8 py-3 bg-teal text-white font-semibold rounded-full hover:bg-teal/90 transition-colors">
          Browse available dogs
        </a>
        <a href="/apply" class="inline-block px-8 py-3 border-2 border-teal text-teal font-semibold rounded-full hover:bg-teal hover:text-white transition-colors">
          Apply to adopt
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

  </article>
</Layout>
